      "completed_at": "2026-05-30T10:36:06Z",
      "duration_ms": 126000,
      "summary": [
        "Reviewed 4 files: gui/api-knowledge.ts, src/storage/knowledge-store.ts, src/schema/knowledge.ts, gui/server.ts. 0 Critical, 0 High, 1 Medium (defence-in-depth gap — documented), 2 Low findings. Security sign-off: PASS."
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "security_issues": 0
      },
      "comments": [
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "medium",
          "timestamp": "2026-05-30T10:36:06Z",
          "note": "MEDIUM — A03 Injection / A04 Insecure Design (defence-in-depth gap): handleDeleteKnowledge (api-knowledge.ts:265) and handlePromoteKnowledge (api-knowledge.ts:320) accept repository_name as a raw string from query parameters. Neither handler applies PROJECT_SLUG_REGEX at the handler level before passing the value to KnowledgeStoreManager. The storage layer's repositoryStorePath() calls _validateSlug() (knowledge-store.ts:516), which tests against PROJECT_SLUG_REGEX and throws a generic Error for non-conforming slugs — making path traversal impossible in practice. However, this generic Error is not caught as an ApiError, so a malformed slug (e.g. '../evil') would propagate to the server's unhandled-error branch, returning a 500 instead of a 400 VALIDATION_ERROR. Recommendation: add an explicit PROJECT_SLUG_REGEX check in handleDeleteKnowledge and handlePromoteKnowledge (mirroring the pattern already used in KnowledgeMoveBodySchema) to (1) ensure path traversal is rejected at the earliest possible layer, (2) return a semantically correct 400 VALIDATION_ERROR rather than 500, and (3) remove reliance on the storage layer as the sole regex guard for these two handlers. Contrast: handleMoveKnowledge and handleUpdateKnowledge use KnowledgeMoveBodySchema/KnowledgeUpdateBodySchema respectively, which both embed z.string().regex(PROJECT_SLUG_REGEX) — so this gap is specific to the two query-param-based handlers."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-05-30T10:36:06Z",
          "note": "LOW — A01 Broken Access Control (observation): The knowledge API is entirely unauthenticated. All five endpoints (list, update, delete, promote, move) operate purely on ledgerRoot, which is a server-side path supplied at process startup. Since the server binds to localhost only (CORS: Access-Control-Allow-Origin: http://localhost:<port>), and all knowledge data is local developer tooling, the absence of auth is an accepted design trade-off for this tool category. Recorded for awareness; no remediation required for this threat model."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-05-30T10:36:06Z",
          "note": "LOW — A05 Security Misconfiguration (observation): CORS is restricted to http://localhost:<port> (server.ts:104-109) — same-origin only. Security headers are applied on all responses: X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff, X-Frame-Options: DENY, Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin, and a restrictive CSP (default-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'). The server is localhost-only tooling with no TLS required. Configuration is correctly hardened for the intended deployment context."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-05-30T10:36:06Z",
          "note": "LOW — A09 Security Logging (observation): Unhandled errors in the knowledge route handlers are logged to process.stderr with route context (e.g. '[server] Unhandled error in PATCH /api/knowledge/:id: ...'). ApiError instances are mapped to structured JSON error responses without leaking internal details (sendError serialises only {error: {code, message}} — no stack traces). The storage layer's _validateSlug() generic Error, noted above as the medium finding, would currently surface as a 500 with the generic 'An unexpected error occurred.' message — no internal path or slug value is leaked to the client. Logging coverage is adequate for this tool."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "status_changed_at": "2026-05-30T10:26:34Z",
  "last_updated": "2026-05-30T10:36:06Z",
  "handoff_notes": [
    {
      "from_agent": "Developer",
      "to_agent": "QA",
      "timestamp": "2026-05-30T10:30:30Z",
      "notes": [
        "All 6 acceptance criteria are met by the existing api-knowledge.ts implementation. The file uses InsightScope z.enum(['global','repository']) throughout — 'project' scope is rejected at the Zod parse boundary in all mutating handlers. 13 new tests were added to document and prove AC compliance. All 2700 tests green; TypeScript clean.",
        "QA focus areas: (1) AC-4 cross-handler scope=project rejection — verified in new test suite; (2) move identity-check is enforced in handleMoveKnowledge handler logic (not just storage layer) via the pre-check before calling moveInsight(); (3) repository_name validation uses PROJECT_SLUG_REGEX which prevents path traversal."
      ]
    },
    {
      "from_agent": "QA",
      "to_agent": "Security Auditor",
      "timestamp": "2026-05-30T10:33:38Z",
      "notes": [
        "WP-005 passes QA. All 6 AC met and verified by test execution. One low-priority observation for Security Auditor: handleDeleteKnowledge and handlePromoteKnowledge accept raw repository_name strings without applying PROJECT_SLUG_REGEX validation at the handler level (unlike KnowledgeMoveBodySchema which enforces it via Zod). Path traversal is blocked in practice by the storage layer (NOT_FOUND thrown), but there is no explicit input sanitization at the API handler level for these two functions. Recommend the Security Auditor assess whether an explicit regex guard should be added."
      ]
    },
    {
      "from_agent": "Security Auditor",
      "to_agent": "Reviewer",
      "timestamp": "2026-05-30T10:36:06Z",
      "notes": [
        "WP-005 passes security audit. 0 Critical/High findings. One Medium defence-in-depth gap documented: handleDeleteKnowledge and handlePromoteKnowledge rely solely on the storage layer's _validateSlug() for repository_name regex enforcement, while handleUpdateKnowledge and handleMoveKnowledge apply PROJECT_SLUG_REGEX at the Zod schema layer. The gap is safe in practice (path traversal is blocked; storage layer throws), but the inconsistency means a malformed slug returns a 500 instead of a 400. Recommend Reviewer note this for a follow-up hardening ticket. All 6 acceptance criteria confirmed met. Security sign-off granted."
      ]
    }
  ]
}

